WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.710
We will look back and
say, what was most amazing

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.470
about this period is the
speed at which we developed

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.080
the vaccines and tested
then distributed them.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.320
It took us four years
just to identify the virus

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.390
that caused AIDS in the eighties.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.530
So just imagine COVID where
it's four years before we even

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.400
know what is causing the outbreak.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.300
That's what would have

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.610
happened if this just shifted
20 years, 30 years earlier

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.190
in terms of when this outbreak happened.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.450
The rapid advance in vaccine technology

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.630
which is bringing an end

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.530
to the COVID 19 pandemic
is best understood

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.440
in the context of a series
of innovations that more

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.890
than doubled life expectancy
over the last hundred years.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.880
This is the single most
incredible thing that's happened

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.350
in a century, argues the science writer

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.900
Steven Johnson in his new book,
Extra Life, a Short History

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.840
of Living Longer, which is also
running as a series on PBS.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.860
It's one of the greatest

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.060
achievements in human history.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.840
The book explores how innovations

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.690
and epidemiological statistics,
artificial fertilizer,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.250
toilets, and sanitation
systems, vaccines and more have

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.100
allowed billions of people
to flourish until old age.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.260
Author and host Johnson
was a founder of the

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.210
pioneering website, Feed in the 1990s

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.240
and has authored a shelf full of books

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.050
about human progress,
including bestsellers, such

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.700
as The Ghost Map, which
recounted how doctors

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.840
and researchers ended the threat
of cholera in 19th century

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.080
London and Future Perfect,

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.880
which argues that the
modern network world is far

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.850
more resilient than previous iterations.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.770
He talks with reason
about how humans manage to

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.020
massively increase their
lifespan in the 20th century

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.460
and whether we might do
even better in the 21st

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.980
Steven Johnson. Thanks
for talking to Reason.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.260
Thanks for having me.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.020
What's the elevator pitch of Extra Life:

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.100
a Short History of Living Longer.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.630
It's right there on the subtitle, I guess

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.270
but the basic elevator pitch is,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.840
the simple fact of the story

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.940
of life expectancy globally
over the past a hundred years,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.770
if you rewound the clock to the kind

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.280
of end of the Spanish flu
in 1920, life expectancy

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.200
in the United States was about 41.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.670
Globally, it was probably
somewhere around 35 and today

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.760
even in the middle of the worst pandemic

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.740
since then, global life
expectancy is just over 72.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.010
So we have doubled the length

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.280
of the average human life in that period.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.850
And this book begins with
the idea that that is

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.370
the single most important
thing that has happened

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:33.750
over the last hundred years.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.880
If a newspaper came out once a century,

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.190
that would be the headline, I think.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.210
Is it appreciated

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.260
or is it that, you know,
every, at any stage people are

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.380
like partway on that curve, you know?

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.850
So like they never really
get that full view.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.330
Yeah, I think it's not appreciated.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:49.180
I mean, if it was appreciated

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.300
I think we wouldn't be here talking

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.387
about the book because people
would have a sense of it.

00:02:53.387 --> 00:02:56.240
And it's not appreciated
for two reasons, I think.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.220
The first is that it's,
it's progress in the

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.280
in the shape that progress
normally takes, which is

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.890
that it's slow and incremental
and made out of, you know

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.560
hundreds of thousands of
small interventions and slow

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.230
incremental progress is
the least interesting thing

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.500
from a kind of a news
cycle perspective, right?

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.900
It just doesn't get covered.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.790
And secondly, it's unlike other forms

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:24.400
of progress like your
smartphone or a skyscraper.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.030
Those are kind of tangible
objects you can point

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.160
to and say, look, wow, I have
a super computer in my pocket.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.100
That's amazing. That's progress.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.320
With health, progress is measured

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.390
in this bizarre way in non events, right?

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.020
The progress is I didn't die

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:41.750
of smallpox when I was two
or that accident I was in.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.760
I didn't, you know,
die in the car accident

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.640
because seatbelts were invented
and I had to wear them.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.990
And so we don't tend to think

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:52.330
about events that didn't happen,

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.163
[Nick] Right.

00:03:53.163 --> 00:03:54.400
for a reason, they didn't happen, right?

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.520
And so we get this skewed sense

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.190
of where the positive and
big changes are happening.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.520
And it's also interesting, you point out

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.130
particularly in the 19th century

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.870
say in England and in the United States

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.510
and then globally in
different settings that

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.640
with the advent of industrialization,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.670
which is really kind of
undergirding a lot of progress,

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.920
there were predictable,
or not predictable,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.260
there were measurable
declines in health outcomes.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:21.600
Where did that come from?

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.520
Because that also it, I mean,
industrialization and the move

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.760
into cities allows for
greater carrying capacity

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.010
but it actually brought
life down and, you know,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.090
the 19th century England for a while.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.320
You know, it's a funny thing, that period,

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.330
and particularly in England

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.660
but also in the United States

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.720
from about 1750 to 1850,
I keep coming back and

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.330
I've written about it a number of times.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.150
And you know, sometimes
people are like, well

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.430
you're just such a Anglophile.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:52.630
You know, you're just
obsessed with that period.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.540
It's like, no, that period
is really interesting

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.230
because something is happening there

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:58.290
for the first time in the history

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.750
of the world, that is then
going to basically be exported

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:03.010
to the rest of the world.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.650
And some of that is industrialization.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:05.760
Some of that is urbanization,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.350
but it's also a story of a
transformation in health.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.410
And so two things are
happening in that period.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:15.030
The first thing that's
interesting is that some subset

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.970
of the population in England
starts to live longer

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.753
in a steady sustained
way, starting around 1750.

